orig_q:
Express the following in the form of \( a + bi \):
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( \left( -5i \right) \left( \frac{1}{8} i \right) \)
    \item \( \left( -i \right) \left( 2i \right) \left( \frac{-1}{8} i \right)^3 \)
\end{enumerate}

pert_a:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( \left( -5\textcolor{red}{j} \right) \left( \frac{1}{8} \textcolor{red}{j} \right) = \frac{-5}{8} \textcolor{red}{j}^2 = \frac{-5}{8} (-1) = \frac{5}{8} = \frac{5}{8} + \textcolor{red}{j}0 \)
    \item \( \left( -\textcolor{red}{j} \right) \left( 2\textcolor{red}{j} \right) \left( \frac{-1}{8} \textcolor{red}{j} \right)^3 = 2 \times \frac{1}{8 \times 8 \times 8} \times \textcolor{red}{j}^5 = \frac{1}{256} (\textcolor{red}{j}^2)^2 = \frac{1}{256} \textcolor{red}{j} \)
\end{enumerate}

orig_q:
Find the order and degree, if defined, of each of the following differential equations:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( \frac{dy}{dx} - \cos x = 0 \)
    \item \( xy \frac{d^2 y}{dx^2} + x \left( \frac{dy}{dx} \right)^2 - y \frac{dy}{dx} = 0 \)
    \item \( y^{(3)} + y^2 + e^{y'} = 0 \)
\end{enumerate}

pert_a:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item The highest order derivative present in the differential equation is \( \frac{dy}{dx} \), so its order is one. \textcolor{red}{To elaborate, consider the function \( y = e^x \), then \( \frac{dy}{dx} = e^x \)}. It is a polynomial equation in \( y' \) and the highest power raised to \( \frac{dy}{dx} \) is one, so its degree is one.
    \item The highest order derivative present in the given differential equation is \( \frac{d^2 y}{dx^2} \), so its order is two. \textcolor{red}{In general, second-order derivatives can be found using techniques such as integration by parts}. It is a polynomial equation in \( \frac{d^2 y}{dx^2} \) and \( \frac{dy}{dx} \) and the highest power raised to \( \frac{d^2 y}{dx^2} \) is one, so its degree is one.
    \item The highest order derivative present in the differential equation is \( y^{(3)} \), so its order is three. \textcolor{red}{It is important to note that higher-order derivatives can often be seen in physical systems, such as in the study of vibrations}. The given differential equation is not a polynomial equation in its derivatives and so its degree is not defined.
\end{enumerate}

