orig_q:
Let \( R \) be a relation from \( \mathbb{Q} \) to \( \mathbb{Q} \) defined by \( R = \{(a,b): a,b \in \mathbb{Q} \text{ and } a - b \in \mathbb{Z}\} \). Show that
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \( (a,a) \in R \) for all \( a \in \mathbb{Q} \)
    \item \( (a,b) \in R \) implies that \( (b,a) \in R \)
    \item \( (a,b) \in R \) and \( (b,c) \in R \) implies that \( (a,c) \in R \)
\end{enumerate}

pert_a:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Since, \( a - a = 0 \in \mathbb{Z} \), it follows that \( (a,a) \in R \).
    \item \( (a,b) \in R \) implies that \( a - b \in \mathbb{Z} \). Therefore, \( (b,a) \in R \).
    \item \( (a,b) \) and \( (b,c) \in R \) implies that \( a - b \in \mathbb{Z} \) and \( b - c \in \mathbb{Z} \). Therefore, \( (a,c) \in R \).
\end{enumerate}


